{# DECIDED: the CrowdSec agent sidecars' :6060 grant admits prometheus from the observability
   namespace, so it stays off the callers model.
   The callers model (`netpol_from` / `netpol_fences`, macro in
   ansible/templates/networkpolicy.yml.j2) renders one TCP port admitting same-namespace `app:`
   peers, and its header rules out growing it to absorb another shape. Census: #3701.
#}
{# One additive policy per pod in `crowdsec_k8s_sidecar_agents` (group_vars/all.yml), the map
   the sidecar's AGENT_USERNAME, the LAPI registration loop and observability's
   `crowdsec-<app>-agent` scrape jobs also read. Adding an app to that map renders its grant
   here, so no app's own policy carries a hand-written :6060 rule (#4088).

   The grant and the scrape job only work as a pair: without the grant the job reads
   `up == 0` forever. Nothing but prometheus may reach :6060, because agent metrics enumerate
   every parser and scenario the pod runs, which is reconnaissance for an attacker.

   The sidecar carries its host pod's labels, so the policy selects `app: <app>` and the
   grant reaches the agent and nothing else only because 6060 is the agent's port.

   A policy of its own rather than a rule in the app's policy, because policies are additive
   and an extra policy admits without fencing anything new. That holds only for a pod some
   other ingress policy already selects: on a pod nothing else selects, this policy would be
   the first, and it would isolate the pod down to :6060. Every pod here is selected by
   `baseline-ingress` (namespace scope, or label scope through `netpol-baseline: enforced`)
   or by a bespoke fence of its own. `baseline-ingress` already admits prometheus on every
   port, so this grant is what keeps :6060 reachable on a pod that leaves the baseline.

   prometheus lives in the observability namespace, so namespaceSelector and podSelector are
   siblings in ONE from item, which ANDs them. As separate items they would OR, admitting every
   pod in observability and every prometheus anywhere.

   Renders allow-all when the flag is false, so `netpol_baseline_enforced` is a real rollback
   lever. `| bool` for the string trap. Both explained in full in
   networkpolicy-traefik.yaml.j2. #}
{% for app in crowdsec_k8s_sidecar_agents %}
---
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: {{ app }}-crowdsec-agent-metrics
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: {{ app }}
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{% if netpol_baseline_enforced | bool %}
    - from:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: observability
          podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: prometheus
      ports:
        - port: 6060
          protocol: TCP
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
